/** Tamar section 20.8: English prompt rules R1-R8, byte-exact (M2a.7: R8 keeps its indented sub-bullets, one per line). */
export const M2A6_PROMPT_RULES: ReadonlyArray<{ id: string; text: string }> = [{"id":"R1","text":"R1 \u2014 Differentiator across the site. Put the owner's differentiator in the home hero and in the hero of the one service it fits best, and nowhere else in a hero. Every other hero leads with the pain of that specific service. Don't use synonyms or paraphrases to repeat it; any phrasing of the same idea counts as a repeat."},{"id":"R2","text":"R2 \u2014 Scene. Write the about-body scene in the second person, present tense: the reader, in his home, now. Never narrate a past job, a named or unnamed past customer, or something the owner did on a specific past occasion. Every page of the site gets a different scene: a different room, moment or failure that belongs to that page's service. The same scene in new words doesn't count. Don't open two pages with the same \"you are standing in\u2026\" frame. End on what the customer gets, never on a lesson, a motto or a principle."},{"id":"R3","text":"R3 \u2014 \"Before\" crutch. Use \"before I start / before I touch / before every job\" constructions at most three times per page. Show the order of the work with a concrete sequence instead."},{"id":"R4","text":"R4 \u2014 Sticky bar. The sticky bar names the customer's problem or the service, plus an action: problem-word question plus action, or \"tell me what the [appliance] is doing\". Never a bare action on its own."},{"id":"R5","text":"R5 \u2014 Service cards (M2a.7). Write all service cards in one shape: short first-person action phrases. That means present-tense verbs of what the owner does, in order, on every card of the page. Don't mix in noun chains (a list of verbal nouns) or passive phrasing. Don't start more than one card with the same three words."},{"id":"R6","text":"R6 \u2014 Trade accuracy. Use the correct trade term and the correct safety action. An AC unit has a condensate drain, never sewage. In a flood the main water valve is closed, not opened. When two verbs share one object, check that both take the same preposition."},{"id":"R7","text":"R7 \u2014 Hero anchor (from Yonatan's H1 anchor rule; he owns the gate). The hero headline opens with the pain hook. The next clause carries the exact service label and the city, as written in the owner's facts. Never drop the service label or the city to make room for the hook."},{"id":"R8","text":"R8 \u2014 Hero hook shape (M2a.7).\n  - The hook is at most 4 words, and it is a question the reader asks himself at that moment, in his own words.\n  - On a planned-stage page (section 19.4), the hook may be the decision question the reader is weighing, instead of a failure. Example of the move, not text to copy: asking whether the home's panel can carry a new charger.\n  - Never invent an abstract failure noun to fill the hook slot, such as an adjective about how hastily something was installed.\n  - After the hook and the anchor clause (R7), a third clause is optional. If used, it is at most 4 words and carries one fact or one action, never a list.\n  - Drop the third clause if the full hero would run past about 60 characters."}];

